---
// Describes the pipeline as it is today. Only state what is true of the
// merged pipeline: when a check, deploy step or dependency bot lands, add it
// here in the same change.
import WithProfile from "../layouts/WithProfile.astro";
import { demoRepo, profile } from "../data/profile";
---

<WithProfile
  title={`How this site is built · ${profile.name}`}
  description="How this site is built: Astro, zero JavaScript, no cookies or tracking, and a hardened, SHA-pinned CI pipeline."
>
  <article class="page">
    <a class="back" href="/#work">← Back to timeline</a>

    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">About this site</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">How this site is built</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        This site is small on purpose, and its build pipeline gets the same care as the infrastructure work it
        describes. Everything on this page is true of the pipeline today. When the pipeline changes, so does this page.
      </p>

      <h2>No JavaScript, no cookies, no tracking</h2>
      <p>
        Every page is plain HTML and CSS. There is no analytics script, no tracking pixel and no cookie banner, because
        there is nothing to consent to. The fonts (IBM Plex Sans and Mono) are served from this site, so reading a page
        makes no requests to anyone else. The colours follow your system's light or dark theme.
      </p>

      <h2>How the content is built</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a> renders every page to static HTML at build time.
        </li>
        <li>
          Case studies are Markdown files with frontmatter checked against a schema. A missing, invalid or unknown field
          fails the build, and so does a case study whose sections differ from the fixed nine-part shape.
        </li>
        <li>
          Diagrams are written as Mermaid inside the Markdown and rendered to inline SVG at build time, so no diagram
          library reaches the browser. Where Mermaid looks too generic, a hand-drawn SVG sits next to the text. A
          diagram without alt text fails the build.
        </li>
        <li>
          Case-study URLs are permanent. A committed registry lists every published slug, and renaming or removing one
          fails the checks.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Checks on every change</h2>
      <p>
        One command, <code>npm run check</code>, runs the same checks on a laptop and in CI, on every pull request and
        every push to <code>main</code>:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Typecheck the whole project.</li>
        <li>Unit-test the check scripts and the diagram plugin.</li>
        <li>Build the site, which runs the schema and section-shape checks.</li>
        <li>Validate the HTML of every built page.</li>
        <li>Fail on any <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> element, in a page or an SVG, and on any JavaScript file in the output.</li>
        <li>Check the site's text colour pairs against WCAG AA contrast, in both themes.</li>
        <li>Check that every registered case-study slug still has a page, and every page is registered.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Checks 4 to 7 run against the built site, the same files a visitor gets, not against the source.</p>

      <h2>The pipeline itself</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Every GitHub Action is pinned to a full commit SHA, with the version in a comment, so a moved tag can't change what runs.</li>
        <li>The workflow token is read-only, and the checkout step doesn't keep its credentials.</li>
        <li>The Node major version is pinned in one file that both local tooling and CI read.</li>
        <li>Every direct npm dependency is pinned to an exact version, and CI installs from the lockfile.</li>
        <li>A newer push to the same branch cancels the older run, and every run has a time limit.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>See it in code</h2>
      <p>
        The case studies on this site describe patterns from real work. The concepts are rebuilt as generic, runnable
        code in <a href={demoRepo.href}>{demoRepo.label}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</WithProfile>
